    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the course was planned by AI agents from research notes on WCAG 2.2 and W3C's guidance on it, on how people with disabilities use the web, on automated testing, and on EU and Swedish law, read from the sources below; an outline was drafted from them and reviewed for facts and question design, and the chapters were then written from it in English and translated from the English into Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French, with criterion names from W3C's authorized translations where one exists. The working notes, the outline and the glossaries are kept with the course's sources, not in the library."@en ,
                 "The course was checked by machine (the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators, its course and Markdown checks, a second SHACL validator, and the course's draft checker, which compares every translation with the English) and by AI agents against the sources; it has had no human review."@en ,
                 "Selection: what a web developer, designer, content writer or public-sector employee needs to make content meet WCAG 2.2 at level AA and to know what the law asks: who meets barriers on the web and the tools people use; how WCAG is built, its levels and conformance requirements, and what changed in 2.2; the success criteria that matter most in practice, principle by principle, with their exceptions and with some AAA criteria named beside their A or AA counterparts; name, role and value, status messages and the first rule of ARIA; what automated tools can and can't find, and WCAG-EM's steps; and the Web Accessibility Directive, accessibility statements and monitoring, EN 301 549, the European Accessibility Act and Swedish law. The sites, shops and municipalities in the examples are invented, and their addresses end in .example. Left out on purpose: questions that ask for a criterion's number alone; every criterion one by one, the techniques and what a conformance claim must contain; any share of problems that tools find, since W3C gives none and vendors' figures differ; WAI's user stories, which are W3C's own creative work; EN 301 549's version numbers and the date a newer version is cited, which would date quickly, so the course teaches the rule (only a cited version gives the presumption of conformity) instead; and the law of countries other than Sweden."@en ,
                 "Structure: 9 chapters of 29 steps in all. Each step is a short theory text in Markdown, read in three chunks (two in four steps), seven of them with a short HTML or CSS example, and three multiple-choice questions on it (two in two steps); each chapter has review questions that combine its steps and are asked only in its final review (two in chapters 1, 2 and 6, three in every other): 85 step questions and 24 review questions, 109 questions in all. Every question has one correct option (the card's back) and three wrong options (its distractors) of similar length and form, each with a note on why it is wrong. Every text is in English, Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French; the code is the same in every language."@en ,
                 "Licensing: the course is CC0 1.0, like most of the library. Its theory, questions, options, notes and examples are in the course's own words. WCAG 2.2 is under the W3C Document License, which allows copying it whole but no modifications or derivative works, so nothing from it is adapted or translated: the course restates its facts in its own words and quotes only the names of success criteria, in English and, in French, German and Swedish, as W3C's authorized translations give them. The WAI pages point to a permissive license in their footers, but W3C's Permission to Use WAI Material puts unmarked WAI material under the Document License; the course takes that stricter reading and uses them for facts only, with two short phrases quoted and attributed in the text. Using ARIA is under the W3C Software and Document License, but its rules are only restated. The EU legal texts are reusable with acknowledgement under Commission Decision 2011/833/EU, and the Commission's page is CC BY 4.0: both are credited in the sources below and used for facts only. EN 301 549 itself was neither read nor quoted; the Swedish statutes and the pages of DIGG, PTS and AccessibleEU were used for facts only. The glossaries also drew on terms in established use, such as unofficial translations of WCAG and the EU texts' own language versions, for terms only."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-31T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the whole release, with the library checker (npm run library:check), which validates it against Solid Memo's shapes (library deck format 6, card format 5, chapter, step and distractor format 1), DCAT-AP and SKOS, with the reference data, checks the course's outline by its rules and its Markdown by the rules for a release, and with a second, independent SHACL validator (pySHACL, npm run crosscheck) over the same shapes; before the build, the course's draft checker compared every chapter translation with the English for its structure (steps, questions, options and Markdown flags) and for the same number of theory chunks in every step."@en ,
                 "Outcome: the release conforms to every shape, profile and rule the checker applies to it, and the crosscheck agrees."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-31T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Outline: facts and question design (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the outline checked by two AI agents, one that tried to refute every teaching point, answer and wrong option against the sources, and one for the questions' design (one answer, recall from the front alone, options of the same form, review questions that combine a chapter's steps, coverage of the planned topics); not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 69 findings (24 on facts, 45 on design, 10 of them the same problem found by both agents), all fixed in the outline before the chapters were written, one in another way than suggested and one in part; among them a CAPTCHA question set on a login page, where a stricter criterion also applies, a question on EN 301 549's version that would turn false once a newer version is cited, a wrong option that was in fact the real reason 4.1.1 Parsing was dropped, review questions that drew on one step only, and wrong options a learner could rule out without knowing the material."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-31T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Chapter text in English: facts, design and language (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: each chapter as written, reviewed by two AI agents: one for every fact, example, answer and wrong option against the sources, and for wording too close to W3C's text; one for the questions' design, each front read alone first, and for the English, the Markdown and the release's format rules; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 118 findings across the nine chapters (65 on facts, 53 on design and language), all fixed; among them a wrong option that was defensible, a wrong count of the criteria 2.2 added under Understandable, a code example that contradicted the text beside it, an outdated rule for links told apart by color alone, a few sentences that followed W3C's wording too closely and were rewritten, and a paragraph on EN 301 549 that would have gone stale; six review questions were added so that every chapter's review combines its steps."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-31T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Course as a whole in English (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the nine chapters read together by an AI agent, for facts taught twice, contradictions, terms used two ways, forward references and promises not kept, and for every question's options (backs that are the longest or stand out by form, and fronts that do not work as flip cards); not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 12 findings, all fixed; among them a note that contradicted what an earlier chapter said ISO/IEC 40500 is, \"programmatic name\" and \"accessible name\" used for the same thing, a front that asked for the lowest level where the highest was meant, and backs that were often the longest option: 23 backs were shortened and 16 wrong options lengthened, so that the back is now the longest in 37 of 109 questions instead of 55, mostly by a few characters."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-31T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Translations into Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: for each language, a glossary of the course's terms drawn up first by an AI agent, with criterion names from W3C's authorized translations (French from WCAG 2.2, German and Swedish from WCAG 2.0) and otherwise from established use or in the course's own words, and legal terms from the EU texts' own language versions; each chapter translated from the English by an AI agent, then reviewed for fidelity, by translating every text back into English and comparing it with the English, and as a native editor for natural language, terminology and typography; then each language's nine chapters read together by an AI agent for consistency; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 505 findings across the 45 chapter translations (Swedish 115, Korean 78, German 123, Spanish 84, French 105), all fixed in the text, among them calques, unidiomatic or wrong-sense wording, terms that strayed from the glossary, and options whose length or form changed in translation; the consistency pass then found 48 more (Swedish 10, Korean 13, German 9, Spanish 8, French 8), such as one English term rendered two ways in different chapters, and evened out terms and voice within each language."@en .

# The sources (W3C PROV-O): what the course took from each, and on what license.
<https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/>
    dcterms:title "Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2" ;
    dcterms:creator "W3C (Accessibility Guidelines Working Group)" ;
    rdfs:comment "The page's header: \"Copyright © 2020-2024 World Wide Web Consortium. W3C® liability, trademark and document use rules apply.\" The document use rules are the W3C Document License (https://www.w3.org/copyright/document-license/), which allows copying and distributing the document but says: \"No right to create modifications or derivatives of W3C documents is granted pursuant to this license\", apart from software and its documentation. So nothing from it is adapted or translated: facts only, restated in the course's own words, nothing copied but the names of success criteria. Reliability: normative (W3C Recommendation of 5 October 2023, as updated on 12 December 2024). Read in October 2026. Used for: the principles, guidelines, success criteria, their levels and exceptions in chapters 2 to 8, the conformance requirements and what changed in 2.2 in chapter 2, and the four principles the Web Accessibility Directive names in chapter 9."@en .

<https://www.w3.org/standards/history/WCAG22/>
    dcterms:title "WCAG 2.2: publication history" ;
    dcterms:creator "W3C" ;
    rdfs:comment "Footer: \"Copyright © 2026 World Wide Web Consortium. W3C® liability, trademark and permissive license rules apply.\" Used for facts only: nothing copied. Reliability: authoritative (W3C's own record). Read in October 2026. Used for: the dates of WCAG 2.2's two Recommendations (October 2023 and December 2024) in chapter 2."@en .

<https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/>
    dcterms:title "Understanding WCAG 2.2" ;
    dcterms:creator "W3C Web Accessibility Initiative (WAI), Accessibility Guidelines Working Group" ;
    rdfs:comment "Footer: \"W3C® liability, trademark and permissive license rules apply unless otherwise noted. See Permission to Use WAI Material.\" That page (https://www.w3.org/WAI/about/using-wai-material/) says: \"Unless otherwise indicated, all WAI material not covered by another license is published under the W3C Document License\", which \"does not allow modifications or the creation of derivative works\". The course takes that stricter reading: facts only, restated in its own words, nothing copied. Reliability: informative but authoritative (W3C's own explanation of each criterion). Read in October 2026, about sixty criterion pages. Used for: the intent, examples and exceptions of the success criteria in chapters 2 to 8, and why 4.1.1 Parsing was made obsolete in chapter 2."@en .

<https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/>
    dcterms:title "WCAG 2 Overview" ;
    dcterms:creator "W3C Web Accessibility Initiative (WAI)" ;
    rdfs:comment "Same WAI footer and Permission to Use WAI Material as Understanding WCAG 2.2; the stricter reading is taken. Used for facts only: nothing copied. Reliability: authoritative (W3C WAI, updated 17 September 2026). Read in October 2026. Used for: WCAG's versions, W3C's advice to use the latest one, and WCAG 2.2's approval as ISO/IEC 40500, in chapter 2."@en .

<https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/new-in-22/>
    dcterms:title "What's New in WCAG 2.2" ;
    dcterms:creator "W3C Web Accessibility Initiative (WAI)" ;
    rdfs:comment "Same WAI footer and Permission to Use WAI Material as Understanding WCAG 2.2; the stricter reading is taken. Used for facts only: nothing copied, and none of its user stories. Reliability: authoritative (W3C WAI). Read in October 2026. Used for: the criteria new in 2.2, their levels and the groups of users they aim at, in chapter 2."@en .

<https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/faq/>
    dcterms:title "WCAG 2 FAQ" ;
    dcterms:creator "W3C Web Accessibility Initiative (WAI)" ;
    rdfs:comment "Same WAI footer and Permission to Use WAI Material as Understanding WCAG 2.2; the stricter reading is taken. Used for facts only: nothing copied. Reliability: authoritative (W3C WAI). Read in October 2026. Used for: what the December 2024 update changed, and that 4.1.1 Parsing is treated as always met for HTML and XML in WCAG 2.0 and 2.1, with other criteria catching the markup errors that still matter, in chapter 2."@en .

<https://www.w3.org/WAI/people-use-web/>
    dcterms:title "How People with Disabilities Use the Web" ;
    dcterms:creator "W3C Web Accessibility Initiative (WAI), Education and Outreach Working Group" ;
    rdfs:comment "Footer: \"Copyright © 2026 World Wide Web Consortium. W3C® liability, trademark and permissive license rules apply unless otherwise noted. See Permission to Use WAI Material.\"; that page puts unmarked WAI material under the W3C Document License, and the course takes that stricter reading. Used for facts only: nothing copied, and none of its user stories or their names; the course's examples are its own. Reliability: authoritative (W3C WAI, updated 25 June 2024). Read in October 2026, with its pages Diverse Abilities and Barriers and Tools and Techniques. Used for: the kinds of disability, age-related, temporary and situational limitations, and the tools people use (screen readers, braille displays, magnification, switches, speech input, head and eye tracking, captions), in chapter 1."@en .

<https://www.w3.org/TR/using-aria/>
    dcterms:title "Using ARIA" ;
    dcterms:creator "W3C (Accessible Rich Internet Applications Working Group)" ;
    dcterms:license <https://www.w3.org/copyright/software-license-2023/> ;
    rdfs:comment "The page's header: \"Copyright © 2026 World Wide Web Consortium. W3C® liability, trademark and permissive document license rules apply.\", linked to the W3C Software and Document License, which allows copying and changing with its notice. Content taken: its rules of ARIA restated in the course's own words; nothing copied. Reliability: a W3C Discontinued Draft (24 February 2026), kept for reference, not a standard, as the course says. Read in October 2026. Used for: the first rule of ARIA and the rules on interactive ARIA controls and hidden focusable elements, in chapter 8."@en .

<https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/practices/read-me-first/>
    dcterms:title "ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG): Read Me First" ;
    dcterms:creator "W3C Web Accessibility Initiative (WAI)" ;
    rdfs:comment "WAI footer, \"permissive license rules apply unless otherwise noted. See Permission to Use WAI Material.\"; the course takes the stricter reading of that page (the W3C Document License). Content taken: one heading quoted and attributed in the text (\"No ARIA is better than Bad ARIA\"); otherwise facts only, in the course's own words. Reliability: authoritative guidance (W3C WAI). Read in October 2026. Used for: why a role is a promise of behavior, in chapter 8."@en .

<https://www.w3.org/WAI/test-evaluate/tools/selecting/>
    dcterms:title "Selecting Web Accessibility Evaluation Tools" ;
    dcterms:creator "W3C Web Accessibility Initiative (WAI)" ;
    rdfs:comment "WAI footer and Permission to Use WAI Material as above; the stricter reading is taken. Content taken: one short phrase quoted and attributed in the text (\"human judgement is required\"); otherwise facts only. Reliability: authoritative (W3C WAI, updated 13 May 2024). Read in October 2026. Used for: what automated tools can and can't check, and that they give false and misleading results, in chapter 9."@en .

<https://www.w3.org/WAI/test-evaluate/>
    dcterms:title "Evaluating Web Accessibility Overview" ;
    dcterms:creator "W3C Web Accessibility Initiative (WAI)" ;
    rdfs:comment "WAI footer and Permission to Use WAI Material as above (only its videos are marked CC BY 4.0, and none was used); the stricter reading is taken. Used for facts only: nothing copied. Reliability: authoritative (W3C WAI). Read in October 2026. Used for: evaluating early and throughout, and involving people with disabilities, in chapter 9."@en .

<https://www.w3.org/WAI/test-evaluate/conformance/wcag-em/>
    dcterms:title "WCAG-EM Overview: Website Accessibility Conformance Evaluation Methodology" ;
    dcterms:creator "W3C Web Accessibility Initiative (WAI)" ;
    rdfs:comment "WAI footer and Permission to Use WAI Material as above; the stricter reading is taken. Used for facts only: nothing copied. Reliability: authoritative (a W3C Group Note's overview; it adds no requirements to WCAG). Read in October 2026. Used for: what WCAG-EM is for and its five steps, in chapter 9, without a version number."@en .

<https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2016/2102/oj>
    dcterms:title "Directive (EU) 2016/2102 on the accessibility of the websites and mobile applications of public sector bodies" ;
    dcterms:creator "European Parliament and Council of the European Union" ;
    rdfs:comment "EU legislation, reusable under Commission Decision 2011/833/EU: \"All documents shall be available for reuse\" (Art. 4), on conditions such as \"the obligation for the reuser to acknowledge the source of the documents\" (Art. 6(2)). Credit: © European Union, https://eur-lex.europa.eu. EUR-Lex answered with a bot challenge, so the same Official Journal text was read from the EU Publications Office (CELEX 32016L2102). Used for facts only: nothing copied. Reliability: the law itself. Read in October 2026. Used for: chapter 9: which bodies, sites and apps the directive covers and what it leaves out, its requirement, the presumption of conformity, disproportionate burden, accessibility statements and monitoring."@en .

<https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dec_impl/2018/1523/oj>
    dcterms:title "Commission Implementing Decision (EU) 2018/1523 establishing a model accessibility statement" ;
    dcterms:creator "European Commission" ;
    rdfs:comment "Reusable with acknowledgement under Commission Decision 2011/833/EU, as above. Credit: © European Union, https://eur-lex.europa.eu. Read from the Publications Office (CELEX 32018D1523). Used for facts only: nothing copied. Reliability: the law itself. Read in October 2026. Used for: what an accessibility statement must contain, where to find it, its three compliance statuses and how it was prepared, in chapter 9."@en .

<https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dec_impl/2018/1524/oj>
    dcterms:title "Commission Implementing Decision (EU) 2018/1524 establishing a monitoring methodology" ;
    dcterms:creator "European Commission" ;
    rdfs:comment "Reusable with acknowledgement under Commission Decision 2011/833/EU, as above. Credit: © European Union, https://eur-lex.europa.eu. Read from the Publications Office (CELEX 32018D1524). Used for facts only: nothing copied. Reliability: the law itself. Read in October 2026. Used for: in-depth and simplified monitoring, and how far simplified monitoring leans on automated tests, in chapter 9."@en .

<https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dec_impl/2018/2048/oj>
    dcterms:title "Commission Implementing Decision (EU) 2018/2048 on the harmonised standard for websites and mobile applications" ;
    dcterms:creator "European Commission" ;
    rdfs:comment "Reusable with acknowledgement under Commission Decision 2011/833/EU, as above. Credit: © European Union, https://eur-lex.europa.eu. Read from the Publications Office. Used for facts only: nothing copied. Reliability: the law itself. Read in October 2026. Used for: that EN 301 549 is the harmonised standard under the Web Accessibility Directive, in chapter 9."@en .

<https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dec_impl/2021/1339/oj>
    dcterms:title "Commission Implementing Decision (EU) 2021/1339 amending Implementing Decision (EU) 2018/2048" ;
